bsection{1. Overview}\\label{overview}\n\nThis lecture covers the fundamentals of \\textbf{database technologies},\nincluding key concepts like \\textbf{operational vs.~analytics data},\n\\textbf{data movement methods (ETL/ELT)}, \\textbf{scalability}, and\n\\textbf{database types (relational, NoSQL, distributed SQL)}. It also\nexplores the evolution of \\textbf{database as a service (DBaaS)} and its\nrole in modern cloud computing.\n\n\\begin{center}\\rule{0.5\\linewidth}{0.5pt}\\end{center}\n\n\\subsection{2. Key Concepts and\nDefinitions}\\label{key-concepts-and-definitions}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Database Management System (DBMS)}: Software that manages\n  databases, enabling data storage, retrieval, and management.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Database Instance}: A logical container of data created by\n  users via a DBMS.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Database Schema}: The structure of data, defining tables,\n  columns, and relationships.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OLTP (Online Transaction Processing)}: Systems handling\n  high-volume, low-latency transactions (e.g., operational data).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OLAP (Online Analytical Processing)}: Systems for complex\n  queries and historical analysis (e.g., analytics data).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ETL (Extract, Transform, Load)}: A process for moving and\n  transforming data between systems.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ELT (Extract, Load, Transform)}: A variant of ETL where data\n  is loaded first and transformed later.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Scale-Up}: Adding resources to a single server to improve\n  performance.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Scale-Out}: Distributing workloads across multiple servers for\n  scalability.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{NoSQL}: Non-relational databases that prioritize scalability\n  over strict consistency.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Distributed SQL}: Databases combining relational consistency\n  with distributed scalability.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{In-Memory Database}: Stores data in RAM for faster access,\n  often paired with NoSQL.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Time Series Database}: Optimized for storing and querying\n  time-stamped data (e.g., IoT).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Graph Database}: Stores data as networks of nodes and edges\n  for relationship-heavy use cases.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{center}\\rule{0.5\\linewidth}{0.5pt}\\end{center}\n\n\\subsection{3. Detailed Notes}\\label{detailed-notes}\n\n\\subsubsection{\\texorpdfstring{\\textbf{Operational vs.~Analytics\nData}}{Operational vs.~Analytics Data}}\\label{operational-vs.-analytics-data}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Operational Data}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Used for \\textbf{day-to-day business operations}.\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{OLTP}-oriented: High-volume, small transactions with\n    \\textbf{low latency}.\\\\\n  \\item\n    Prioritizes \\textbf{consistency} and \\textbf{real-time updates}.\\\\\n  \\item\n    Best suited for \\textbf{relational databases (SQL)}.\\\\\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Analytics Data}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Used for \\textbf{business intelligence} and \\textbf{long-term\n    decision-making}.\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{OLAP}-oriented: Complex queries, historical analysis, and\n    multi-dimensional data.\\\\\n  \\item\n    Stored in \\textbf{data warehouses} or \\textbf{data lakes}.\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{\\texorpdfstring{\\textbf{Data Movement and\nTransformation}}{Data Movement and Transformation}}\\label{data-movement-and-transformation}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Batch vs.~Real-Time Processing}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Batch}: Scheduled data transfers (e.g., nightly ETL).\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Real-Time}: Continuous data streams (e.g., IoT sensors).\\\\\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ETL vs.~ELT}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{ETL}: Transform data before loading (common for structured\n    systems like data warehouses).\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{ELT}: Load data first, then transform (common for flexible\n    systems like data lakes).\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{\\texorpdfstring{\\textbf{Scalability and Database\nTypes}}{Scalability and Database Types}}\\label{scalability-and-database-types}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Scale-Up vs.~Scale-Out}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Scale-Up}: Add more resources to a single server (e.g., RAM,\n    CPU).\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Scale-Out}: Distribute data across multiple nodes (e.g.,\n    distributed databases).\\\\\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Database Types}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Relational (SQL)}:\n\n    \\begin{itemize}\n    \\tightlist\n    \\item\n      Structured data in tables.\\\\\n    \\item\n      Strong consistency, but limited scalability.\\\\\n    \\item\n      Ideal for OLTP.\\\\\n    \\end{itemize}\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{NoSQL}:\n\n    \\begin{itemize}\n    \\tightlist\n    \\item\n      Flexible schemas (e.g., key-value, document, columnar, graph).\\\\\n    \\item\n      Prioritizes scalability over strict consistency.\\\\\n    \\item\n      Examples: MongoDB (document), Cassandra (columnar), Neo4j\n      (graph).\\\\\n    \\end{itemize}\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Distributed SQL}:\n\n    \\begin{itemize}\n    \\tightlist\n    \\item\n      Combines SQL’s consistency with distributed scalability (e.g.,\n      Google Spanner).\\\\\n    \\end{itemize}\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{In-Memory Databases}:\n\n    \\begin{itemize}\n    \\tightlist\n    \\item\n      Store data in RAM for speed (e.g., Redis).\\\\\n    \\end{itemize}\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Time Series Databases}:\n\n    \\begin{itemize}\n    \\tightlist\n    \\item\n      Optimized for temporal data (e.g., InfluxDB).\n    \\end{itemize}\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{\\texorpdfstring{\\textbf{Database as a Service\n(DBaaS)}}{Database as a Service (DBaaS)}}\\label{database-as-a-service-dbaas}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Evolution}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Traditional databases faced scalability and cost challenges.\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{DBaaS} offers cloud-based solutions (e.g., Azure, AWS,\n    Google Cloud).\\\\\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Benefits}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Reduced infrastructure management.\\\\\n  \\item\n    Pay-as-you-go pricing.\\\\\n  \\item\n    Scalability and global availability.\\\\\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Cloud Providers}:\n\n  \\begin{itemize}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{AWS}: Aurora, RDS, DynamoDB.\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Azure}: Cosmos DB, SQL Database.\\\\\n  \\item\n    \\textbf{Google Cloud}: Bigtable, Cloud SQL.\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{center}\\rule{0.5\\linewidth}{0.5pt}\\end{center}\n\n\\subsection{4. Glossary}\\label{glossary}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DBMS}: Database Management System (software for managing\n  databases).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OLTP}: Online Transaction Processing (high-volume, low-latency\n  transactions).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{OLAP}: Online Analytical Processing (complex queries for\n  historical analysis).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ETL}: Extract, Transform, Load (data movement process).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{ELT}: Extract, Load, Transform (variant of ETL for flexible\n  systems).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Scale-Up}: Adding resources to a single server.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Scale-Out}: Distributing workloads across multiple nodes.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{NoSQL}: Non-relational databases with flexible schemas.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Distributed SQL}: SQL databases that scale horizontally.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{In-Memory Database}: Stores data in RAM for fast access.\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Time Series Database}: Optimized for temporal data (e.g.,\n  IoT).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Graph Database}: Stores data as nodes and edges (e.g., social\n  networks).\\\\\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DBaaS}: Database as a Service (cloud-based database\n  solutions).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\begin{center}\\rule{0.5\\linewidth}{0.5pt}\\end{center}\n\n\\subsection{5. Practice Questions}\\label{practice-questions}\n\n\\subsubsection{\\texorpdfstring{\\textbf{Multiple Choice Questions\n(15)}}{Multiple Choice Questions (15)}}\\label{multiple-choice-questions-15}\n\n\\textbf{Q1:} What is the primary purpose of operational data?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Long-term business analysis\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Real-time transaction processing\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Data warehousing\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Graph analytics\\\\\n    \\textbf{C) B (Correct)}\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q2:} Which database type is best suited for OLAP workloads?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Relational (SQL)\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    NoSQL (document)\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Time Series\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Graph\\\\\n    \\textbf{C) A (Correct)}\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q3:} What is the main difference between ETL and ELT?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    ETL loads data first, ELT transforms first\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    ETL transforms data first, ELT loads first\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    ETL is for real-time data, ELT for batch data\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    ETL is for structured data, ELT for unstructured data\\\\\n    \\textbf{C) B (Correct)}\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q4:} Which database model is ideal for storing data with complex\nrelationships (e.g., social networks)?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Key-Value\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Document\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Graph\\\\\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Columnar\\\\\n    \\textbf{C) C (Correct)}\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q5:} What is the primary advantage of a distributed SQL\ndatabase?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\lab